.TH GXWI-SYSTEM-SETTINGS 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-system-settings 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-system-settings \- this machine's time, language, name and startup, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-system-settings
.SH DESCRIPTION
System Settings shows the machine's own settings in four sections down the side.
A switch or a choice from a list applies the moment it is made; one that cannot be is put back, and the line along the bottom says why.
What is typed applies when its button is pressed: Apply, Save or Set Clock.
What is shown is read again every five seconds, so a change made elsewhere appears.
.PP
Time and Date shows the time here and how well it is kept, asked of timed.
It sets the time zone, whether the time is set automatically, the time servers and how often they are asked, all in \%Machine\eSystem\eTime, which timed watches.
With automatic time off, Set Clock asks timed to set the clock, which needs timed's control right.
.PP
Language and Keyboard sets the language and formats every session starts in, in \%Machine\eSystem\eLocale, from the locales installed, and the console's keyboard layout, in \%Machine\eSystem\eConsole.
A new layout is loaded at once by restarting the console-keymap service, where the person can restart it, and otherwise at the next boot.
.PP
Startup and Shutdown says how this boot went, asked of peinit.
It sets how long peinit waits at each stage, in \%Machine\eSystem\eBoot; which local account, if any, the console signs in without a password; and, on a machine with the dynamic-boot feature, three flags on the kernel command line the next boot image is made from: when to start in recovery, what peinit writes to the console, and whether every boot starts in safe mode.
.PP
About shows the release, the kernel, the hardware and the disks, and sets the machine's name, in \%Machine\eSystem\eNetwork, which netd applies.
.PP
Whether the person can change each is asked of the system, by opening the key or file for writing, never worked out from who they are.
As shipped, only Administrators can change any of it.
Anyone else sees what they can, and the window says once why they cannot change it.
A person's own settings are My Settings', and the desktop's are Desktop Settings'.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The application edits the registry settings owned by timed, netd, peinit, locale, and console components.
Use each consumer's registry manual for its types, defaults, and application timing.
.SH SECURITY
Each setting uses its registry or file access check.
Immediate daemon operations also require their control rights.
Machine settings do not replace the credentials or locale of existing sessions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-system-settings.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as System Settings.
.TP
.I /lcl/etc/boot/cmdline
The kernel command line the next boot image is made from.
.TP
.I /usr/share/zoneinfo/zone1970.tab
The time zones offered.
.TP
.I /usr/share/kbd/keymaps/i386
The console keyboard layouts offered.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-peios/locale
The languages and formats offered.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/os-release
The release shown under About.
.SH SEE ALSO
The time zone, and setting the clock by hand; Configuring time sources; Session locales; Console keyboards and fonts; Boot and boot modes; and The sign-in command, at learn.peios.org.
